{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's proposed evidence for distinguishing genuine reliability from compliance — failure on an edge case absent from the checklist — is actually evidence of a reliability CEILING, not a method for distinguishing within a single evaluation. The problem asks what evidence WITHIN one evaluation separates genuine gain from compliance that tracks the right answer. If the panel correctly answers all items but misses an omitted edge case, you still cannot tell from that single evaluation whether their correct answers reflected genuine reasoning or mere compliance. The edge-case failure only proves the checklist is incomplete; it does not reveal whether the non-edge answers were genuine. A counterexample: a panel that merely complies and happens to get every listed item right — including the edge case if it happens to be on the list — would look identical to a genuinely reliable panel under this criterion.","counterexample":[{"panel":"compliant, gets all listed items right including edge case because it is on the list","distinguishable_from_genuine":false}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"The 'Justification Trace' criterion cannot distinguish genuine reliability from compliance when the panel has been trained to produce justifications. The problem explicitly asks for evidence that separates genuine gain from 'checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer.' If a panel is trained to cite case-specific evidence for each item — a learned discursive template — their justifications will look evidentiary yet remain rote compliance. The target's own countercondition ('trained to hallucinate justifications') is acknowledged but never resolved: the proposed trace would misclassify such a panel as genuinely reliable.","counterexample":[{"panel":"trained to write 'Item 4 present because observation X shows Y' for every item, mechanically","trace":"appears evidentiary, actually compliant"}],"target_alias":"SRC_002"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target ignores a whole class of objectively verifiable tasks where contextual weighting is irrelevant. The problem covers 'tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes' — a broad category including algorithmic verification (e.g., checking whether a numeric checksum matches). The target's own countercondition concedes this, yet the argument still asserts the expert is more reliable. For such tasks a checklist is not a 'lossy compression' but a complete decision procedure, and the expert's gestalt weighting confers no advantage. The generalization 'single expert remains more reliable' is overbroad.","counterexample":[{"task":"verify whether a SHA-256 hash matches a published value","contextual_weight":"none","checklist":"complete and optimal","expert_gestalt_advantage":"zero"}],"target_alias":"SRC_003"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target does not answer the second half of the problem: what evidence within a single evaluation distinguishes genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. It gives a variance-reduction account for why the panel is reliable and two counterconditions, but no diagnostic evidence. A panel that mechanically ticks boxes and happens to be correct would exhibit the same low variance the target cites as the mechanism; variance reduction alone cannot separate genuine gain from compliant tracking.","counterexample":[{"panel":"mechanically ticks every box, achieves low variance, correct on this evaluation","variance":"low, identical to genuine","distinguishable_from_genuine":"not addressed"}],"target_alias":"SRC_004"}]}